# XSH pthread_setcancelstate() and pthread_setcanceltype()
# (pthread_testcancel(), on the same page, is the xsh/pthread_testcancel module)
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# The state and type are read by setting a value and keeping the previous
# one: no interface reads them otherwise. Every oldstate and oldtype
# argument is a valid pointer (the page does not allow a null one).

assertion  XSH.pthread_setcancelstate.001
anchor     functions/pthread_setcancelstate.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.9.5.1
class      required
since      2008
says       The thread running main() and a thread made with
           pthread_create() (default attributes) both start with
           cancelability state PTHREAD_CANCEL_ENABLE and type
           PTHREAD_CANCEL_DEFERRED.
test       t/001-defaults.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_setcancelstate.002
anchor     functions/pthread_setcancelstate.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/pthread_setcancelstate.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2008
says       In a new thread, pthread_setcancelstate() with DISABLE,
           DISABLE, ENABLE, ENABLE returns 0 each time and stores ENABLE,
           DISABLE, DISABLE, ENABLE as the previous state.
test       t/002-state-round-trip.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_setcancelstate.003
anchor     functions/pthread_setcancelstate.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/pthread_setcancelstate.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2008
says       In a new thread, pthread_setcanceltype() with ASYNCHRONOUS,
           ASYNCHRONOUS, DEFERRED, DEFERRED returns 0 each time and stores
           DEFERRED, ASYNCHRONOUS, ASYNCHRONOUS, DEFERRED as the previous
           type.
test       t/003-type-round-trip.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_setcancelstate.004
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.9.5.1
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.9.5.2
anchor     functions/pthread_setcancelstate.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       A thread that disabled cancelability and then set the type
           ASYNCHRONOUS is not canceled by a request while it runs for
           200 ms, nor (back to DEFERRED) at nanosleep() or at read() of
           a pipe holding a byte; after pthread_setcancelstate(ENABLE)
           returns, the next pthread_testcancel() acts on the request.
note       pthread_setcancelstate() is in neither list of cancellation
           points, and 2.9.5.2 forbids adding any, so enabling cannot act
           on the deferred request itself.
test       t/004-disabled-pending.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_setcancelstate.005
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.9.5.1
class      required
since      2008
says       With the default (deferred) type, a thread that receives a
           request while it runs code with no cancellation point
           (pthread_mutex_trylock(), clock_gettime(), sched_yield()) goes
           on for 200 ms more, and is canceled at pthread_testcancel().
test       t/005-deferred-held.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_setcancelstate.006
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.9.5.1
anchor     functions/pthread_setcancelstate.html#DESCRIPTION
class      disputed
since      2008
says       A thread with the type PTHREAD_CANCEL_ASYNCHRONOUS that runs
           pure computation, with no cancellation point, is canceled
           within 10 s of the request.
note       disputed (Q-72): 2.9.5.1 says only that with the asynchronous
           type requests "may be acted upon at any time"; read as a
           permission, a system may still wait for a cancellation point
           and never cancel such a thread. The RATIONALE of
           pthread_setcancelstate() talks of the points "at which a
           thread may be asynchronously canceled", which supports either
           reading. The thread turns DEFERRED now and then to look at the
           clock (only async-cancel-safe calls are made while
           ASYNCHRONOUS).
test       t/006-async-acts.c
